-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Cache Eviction
+
+Eviction uses a 2Q policy that separates sheets by whether they have been reused, so a long one-directional screening cannot displace the comparison working set. Two queues share the single 12 GiB JPEG budget:
+
+- **Probation** — a FIFO queue holding each sheet from its first admission. An entry leaves probation either by eviction from the queue's tail or by promotion to the main queue.
+- **Main** — an LRU queue holding sheets that have been requested again while cached. A hit here refreshes the entry's recency.
+
+Admission and promotion:
+
+- A newly rendered sheet is admitted to the head of the probation queue.
+- A cache hit on a sheet in the probation queue promotes it to the most-recently-used end of the main queue.
+- A cache hit on a sheet in the main queue refreshes its recency within that queue.
+
+A **probation share** sets the probation queue's target size as a fraction of the 12 GiB budget, measured in bytes. Its default is 25%.
+
+When admitting a sheet would exceed the 12 GiB total, reclaim space by repeating the following until the sheet fits: if the probation queue is at or above its probation-share target, or the main queue is empty, evict the probation queue's FIFO tail; otherwise evict the main queue's LRU tail. An evicted sheet's JPEG bytes are deleted and no record of it is retained.
+
+A one-use sheet from a screening pass stays in the probation queue and is evicted from its FIFO tail before any second request could promote it. Sheets already promoted to the main queue by repeated comparison are therefore shielded from the scan.
+
+Eviction never deletes source footage, changes job-ticket semantics, or crosses the worker boundary. This policy leaves the external interface, the four-job render-queue concurrency, and the 12 GiB disk budget unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (FIFO probation queue + LRU main queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Scan resistance decides it: 2Q shields the reused comparison set from one-use screening sheets, where LRU is scan-vulnerable and frequency-with-aging only partial and costlier. Options and criteria in full: `decisions/eviction-policy.md`. |
+| Probation queue measured by bytes, not entry count | Bytes | Fixed entry count | The 12 GiB budget is a byte bound and sheets vary in size by revision, so a byte-measured share tracks the budget directly. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. The 25% probation share is a starting value, not a tuned one; confirm it against pilot screening/comparison traces before relying on the split.
+2. Whether to keep a ghost list of recently evicted keys (full 2Q's second probation list) so a sheet re-requested shortly after eviction promotes straight to the main queue. This design omits it and treats such a request as a fresh first-use admission, to hold bookkeeping down.
+3. Whether queue membership survives a worker restart or is rebuilt cold. This design holds queue metadata in worker memory, so a restart begins with an empty cache; the prior design did not state restart behavior either.
